  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1150 AM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nToday through Wednesday: Dry and Warming\n\nAn rebuilding Omega Block remains the dominant weather feature \nacross the north central CONUS into the middle of next week, \nbringing continued dry weather and sunnier skies. A 1030-mb \nsurface high reflection that currently stretches from Lake \nSuperior southward to the Lower Mississippi River Valley \ngradually strengths and slides to the southeast, being centered \nover the Lower Great Lakes and Ohio River Valley for Monday and \nTuesday.\n\nReturn SSW flow slowly builds into the region over the next 24\nhours and remains in place through midweek before the ridge \nbreaks down. Despite the multiple days of southerly flow, we \nwill be slow to warm with our airmass trajectories coming from\nnorthern Mexico and warmer Gulf air relegated to the coast. \nHighs today will rise into the upper 40s to around 50 and inch\ninto the mid to even upper 50s by midweek.\n\n\nThursday into Saturday: Several Rounds of Rain\n\nThe first of a pair of weather systems arrives for Thursday and\nushers a weak cold front through the region, though with the \nfront tapping into a downslope airmass, impacts on temperatures \nlook to be minimal. The degree of lower to mid tropospheric \nsaturation is also being called into question with the latest \nguidance, with almost half of the available ensembles not \nproducing much of any rainfall with its passage. Those members \nthat do only manage to spit out a few hundredths of rain before \nthe front departs.\n\nAfter the passage of this first wave, a strong northward push \nof higher lower tropospheric theta-e air arrives for Friday and \nthe weekend, setting the stage for a stronger Colorado low to \neject across the north-central CONUS. While the ensembles are  \nin agreement on the general concept for this event, there exist\nsubstantial differences in the upper level wave structure and \nsurface low track, which play a large role in the placement of \nthe heaviest precipitation Friday and Saturday and temperatures \nlater in the weekend. \n\nThe signal for higher rainfall amounts continues to shift more \nnortherly over the last 24 hours, mainly driven by the GEFS and \nEC AI. While the higher amounts are trending north with the more\nnortherly low track, nearly all available ensemble members \nbring rainfall and this is reflected in the higher PoP forecast.\nThe latest LREF probabilities for >0.50\" have fallen to around \n20-40% and >1\" sits at <10%. This northward probability shift \nalso favors the region to stay on the warmer side of the low. As\na result, the probabilities for accumulating snow on the \nbackside of the system have trended further northward. We \ncannot fully rule out some flurries on the backside of the \nstorm depending on the strength and timing of the cold air \nadvection--though the recent probabilities for measurable snow \nhave fallen to 10% or less.",
